A||||9||2birds||Exhaust leak finding, gasket replacement questions||||5||
Z||000000||2birds||02-12-2001||12:21 AM||||So how do you ID the location of an exhaust leak on one of these?  After 13 years of ownwership and 168,000 miles, I finally have one, I think  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">.  I used a 3 foot piece of hose to my ear like a stethoscope, and I thought I heard the leak around the fron manifold to crossover flange, so I tightened it, but the leak is still there.  It also seemed to disappear for the last two weeks, then it came back last Friday.<BR>I can only guess what kind of PITA it will be to remove the front manifold, not to mention broken bolts.  What kind of gaskets work best, and what size are the bolts,  so I can get new ones ahead of time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">?||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||02-12-2001||09:09 AM||||I couldn't find any parts houses arround here that had the right bolts, and the dealership wanted twice as much as the fierostore.com the leake may be in the manifolds them selves, I know mine are starting to go, and I too am fearing that inboard manifold.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||02-12-2001||09:51 AM||||You may have a cracked manifold, if you have the V6.  Also, that donut looking thing that goes between the crossover pipe and manifold could be mostly gone.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||02-12-2001||12:25 PM||||Mine is a definite tick sound, only on acceleration and RPM dependent.  I suspect it is only one cylinder I am hearing.  On the gaskets, the filler between each port is missing now, so I wonder if it may have blown out.<P>I just had the whole thing out of the car back in August, when I did my clutch.  It wasn't leaking then, so I used "If it ain't broke, don't fix it" logic.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I can get Fel-pro gaskets from Advance or Autozone for $22, overnight.   Anybody else use these?||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||02-12-2001||03:46 PM||||i have been talking with Ed Parks about the manifolds, i have a cracked one also.<P>he recomend's using <B>Permatex Ultra Copper</B> for the gasket.  he said it was much better, it forms to the aplication.  <P>Ed also recomends using <B>Saturn</B> intake manifold studs, part number 21006951, and Flange nuts # 11501889, or 2609, or 7036.  it takes 6 bolts, per side and 9 flange nuts... if you have the heat shield.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||12
Z||000005||rogergarrison||02-12-2001||05:12 PM||||Felpro are the ones recommended by turbo kit builders so even better if not. Mines been in for 40K and still fine. Replace any of the bolts- broken off or not with either stainless steel, or grade 8 STUDS WITH NUTS.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||02-12-2001||05:46 PM||||I just checked the dealer, and they want $19 and change for each gasket.  Looks like Fel-Pro has the job.  <P>bHooper, did Ed say to use the Ultra Copper by itself or with the gaskets?<P>I didn't think about using studs for every hole.  Sounds like a plan.  Thanks for the part numbers.  Dealer told me they are all M8 x 1.25, with one special bolt that is M10 on the other end.  I'll check the prices for the Saturn studs tomorrow.<P>This leak is embarassing... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||02-12-2001||10:13 PM||||the saturn studs are cheap... like $0.43 ea, and $0.35 ea for the nuts!<P>as for the ultra copper, it really sounded as if i should just use that.  he emailed it to me, then when i asked him on the phone he said that's all they use!<P>i just need to find a source!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000008||c m west||02-12-2001||10:29 PM||||from my experience, I have found that it is often the exhaust manifolds that crack ( the welds that join the head outlet tube to the main tube ) over time the machine-welded joints become brittle and fatigue from the high heat and exhaust.<P>  It is possible as well that the exhaust leak may be coming from the "doughnut" exhaust gasket just after the O2 sensor. These tend to wear out.<P> if you have removed the exhaust system it must go back in this order to prevent leaks:<BR>1. install manifolds, 2. tighten all 4 bolts that retain the crossover tube, 3. attach the EGR system, 4. tighten the 2 flange bolts that connecter the y-tube to the lower exhaust<P>Eric- West Coast Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||02-13-2001||11:23 PM||||Again, thanks for all of your input.<P>I've never removed the manifolds, although the Y-pipe has been unbolted from them to facilitate transaxle removal twice by me when doing the clutch. I checked the Y-pipe flanges tight the other day.<BR>Never touched the EGR system since the dealer replaced the tube back in '89.<BR>I used a new graphite donut gasket each time I removed the headpipe-back portion for the clutch jobs.  I don't suspect this part to be leaking, based on the slow ticking noise.  Seems to me I would be hearing 6 times as many pulses as a cylinder-specific leak, where this one sounds like my dad's Model T at about 400 RPM.  <P>I'll get it up on jackstands soon and check the donut flange.  Work's been kind of hectic, and the weather's been crappy, too.  I don't think my wife would appreciate me working on it tomorrow, either    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 02-13-2001).]||||reg||1
